/* From React_JS_Interview_Questions_.pdf: React, HTML and CSS question lists */
HUB.add({
  id: 'html', title: 'HTML', short: 'HTML',
  blurb: 'Doctype, semantic tags, block vs inline, HTML5, iframe, viewport and attributes.',
  items: [
{ q: `What is <!DOCTYPE html> in HTML5?`,
  k: `The first line tells the browser "this is a modern web page, read it the modern way".`,
  a: `A declaration (not a tag) at the top of the document that makes the browser use <b>standards mode</b> instead of quirks mode. HTML5's doctype is just <code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</code>; older versions referenced long DTD URLs.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between div and span?`,
  k: `div is a big box that takes the whole row; span is a small sticker inside a line of text.`,
  a: `Both are generic, non-semantic containers. <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> is block-level (starts on a new line, takes full width) and groups sections. <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> is inline (flows within text) and wraps small pieces of text for styling. Prefer semantic elements where one fits.` },
{ q: `What are semantic and non-semantic tags?`,
  k: `Semantic tags have names that say what's inside (header, nav, article); non-semantic ones are plain boxes (div, span).`,
  a: `Semantic elements describe meaning: <code>header</code>, <code>nav</code>, <code>main</code>, <code>section</code>, <code>article</code>, <code>aside</code>, <code>footer</code>, <code>figure</code>, <code>time</code>, <code>button</code>, <code>form</code>. Non-semantic: <code>div</code>, <code>span</code>. Semantics improve accessibility (screen-reader landmarks), SEO and readability.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between HTML and HTML5?`,
  k: `HTML5 is the newer version with built-in video, drawing, storage and smarter page sections.`,
  a: `HTML5 added a simple doctype; semantic elements; native <code>&lt;audio&gt;</code>/<code>&lt;video&gt;</code>; <code>&lt;canvas&gt;</code> and inline SVG; new input types (email, date, range, number) and validation attributes; Web Storage; geolocation, drag and drop, Web Workers, WebSockets and history APIs; and it dropped presentational tags like <code>&lt;font&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;center&gt;</code>. Today "HTML" is a living standard maintained by WHATWG.` },
{ q: `What is the iframe tag?`,
  k: `A window in your page that shows another web page inside it.`,
  a: `<code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code> embeds another document (maps, videos, payment widgets). Security: use <code>sandbox</code>, <code>allow</code> and <code>referrerpolicy</code>; the embedded site can forbid framing with <code>X-Frame-Options</code> / CSP <code>frame-ancestors</code> (clickjacking protection). Add <code>title</code> for accessibility and <code>loading="lazy"</code> for performance. Communicate across frames with <code>postMessage</code>, checking origin.` },
{ q: `What are the formatting tags in HTML?`,
  k: `Tags that make words bold, slanted, underlined, small, crossed out or highlighted.`,
  a: `<code>&lt;b&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;strong&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;i&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;em&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;u&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;mark&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;small&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;del&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;ins&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;s&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;sub&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;sup&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;code&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;blockquote&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;abbr&gt;</code>. Prefer meaning-based tags and do visual styling in CSS.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between <b> and <strong>?`,
  k: `Both look bold, but strong also says "this is important!" to screen readers and search engines.`,
  a: `<code>&lt;b&gt;</code> is stylistic offset text without extra importance. <code>&lt;strong&gt;</code> marks strong importance (semantic). Same for <code>&lt;i&gt;</code> (alternate voice, e.g. terms) vs <code>&lt;em&gt;</code> (stress emphasis).` },
{ q: `What is the viewport meta tag?`,
  k: `It tells phones "use your real screen width, don't zoom out to show a tiny desktop page".`,
  a: `<code>&lt;meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"&gt;</code> sets the layout viewport to the device width so responsive CSS and media queries work on mobile. Don't disable zoom (<code>user-scalable=no</code>) because it hurts accessibility.` },
{ q: `What is an attribute in HTML?`,
  k: `Extra settings written inside a tag, like a name tag or a size.`,
  a: `Name/value pairs in the start tag that configure elements: global attributes (<code>id</code>, <code>class</code>, <code>style</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>lang</code>, <code>hidden</code>, <code>tabindex</code>, <code>data-*</code>, <code>aria-*</code>) and element-specific ones (<code>href</code>, <code>src</code>, <code>alt</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>disabled</code>). Boolean attributes are true when present.` },
{ q: `What are block-level and inline elements?`,
  k: `Block elements are like bricks that each take a whole row; inline elements sit side by side in a sentence.`,
  a: `<b>Block</b> (<code>div</code>, <code>p</code>, <code>h1</code>–<code>h6</code>, <code>ul</code>, <code>section</code>): start on a new line, take full width, respect width/height and vertical margins. <b>Inline</b> (<code>span</code>, <code>a</code>, <code>strong</code>, <code>img</code>): flow with text, width/height don't apply (except replaced elements like img), vertical margins don't push lines. <code>inline-block</code> flows inline but accepts box sizing. CSS <code>display</code> can change any element's behaviour.` },
{ q: `How do you make HTML accessible?`,
  k: `Build the page so everyone can use it: people with screen readers, keyboards only, or poor eyesight.`,
  a: `Semantic elements and landmarks, one <code>h1</code> and a logical heading order, <code>alt</code> text, <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> for every input, real <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>/<code>&lt;a&gt;</code> elements, keyboard focus order and visible focus, <code>lang</code> on html, sufficient colour contrast, ARIA only when native HTML can't express it.` },
  ],
});

HUB.add({
  id: 'css', title: 'CSS', short: 'CSS',
  blurb: 'Selectors, box model, positions, flexbox, media queries, units, responsive design.',
  items: [
{ q: `What is the difference between CSS and CSS3?`,
  k: `CSS3 is the newer CSS with rounded corners, shadows, animations and smart layouts.`,
  a: `CSS3 split CSS into modules and added border-radius, shadows, gradients, transitions, animations, transforms, media queries, flexbox, grid, web fonts, new selectors and more. There's no "CSS4" release; modules evolve independently (container queries, <code>:has()</code>, nesting, cascade layers, custom properties).` },
{ q: `What are the selectors in CSS?`,
  k: `Ways to point at which boxes to paint: by name, by label, by id, or by family relationship.`,
  a: `Universal <code>*</code>, type <code>p</code>, class <code>.btn</code>, id <code>#main</code>, attribute <code>[type="email"]</code>, grouping <code>h1, h2</code>, combinators: descendant <code>a b</code>, child <code>a &gt; b</code>, adjacent sibling <code>a + b</code>, general sibling <code>a ~ b</code>; pseudo-classes <code>:hover</code>, <code>:focus-visible</code>, <code>:nth-child()</code>, <code>:not()</code>, <code>:has()</code>; pseudo-elements <code>::before</code>, <code>::after</code>, <code>::placeholder</code>. Specificity: inline &gt; id &gt; class/attribute/pseudo-class &gt; type; <code>!important</code> overrides (avoid).` },
{ q: `What are pseudo-classes and pseudo-elements?`,
  k: `Pseudo-classes paint a box when it's in a special mood (like hovered); pseudo-elements paint an invisible extra piece (like a decoration before the text).`,
  a: `Pseudo-classes (single colon) target states or positions: <code>:hover</code>, <code>:focus</code>, <code>:checked</code>, <code>:disabled</code>, <code>:first-child</code>, <code>:nth-child(2n)</code>. Pseudo-elements (double colon) style parts or generated content: <code>::before</code>/<code>::after</code> (need <code>content</code>), <code>::first-line</code>, <code>::selection</code>, <code>::marker</code>.` },
{ q: `What is a media query?`,
  k: `A rule that says "if the screen is small, paint it this way".`,
  a: `<code>@media</code> applies styles conditionally on device features: width, orientation, resolution, <code>prefers-color-scheme</code>, <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>, <code>hover</code>/<code>pointer</code>. Write mobile-first (<code>min-width</code>). Container queries (<code>@container</code>) adapt components to their container instead of the viewport.`,
  c: `.grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
@media (min-width: 768px) { .grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); } }
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { * { animation: none; } }` },
{ q: `What are the different position values in CSS?`,
  k: `Static: normal spot. Relative: nudged from its spot. Absolute: placed exactly inside its parent box. Fixed: glued to the screen. Sticky: scrolls, then sticks.`,
  a: `<code>static</code> (default, normal flow). <code>relative</code> (offset from its normal place, still takes space; creates a containing block for absolute children). <code>absolute</code> (removed from flow, positioned relative to the nearest positioned ancestor). <code>fixed</code> (relative to the viewport; stays while scrolling, unless an ancestor has transform). <code>sticky</code> (relative until a scroll threshold, then sticks within its parent). Use <code>z-index</code> with positioned elements and stacking contexts.` },
{ q: `What is the box model, and why do we use box-sizing?`,
  k: `Every box has the stuff inside, padding around it, a border, and space outside. box-sizing decides whether width includes the padding and border.`,
  a: `Content → padding → border → margin. With <code>box-sizing: content-box</code> (default), <code>width</code> covers only content, so padding and border make the box wider. <code>border-box</code> includes padding and border in the width, which makes layouts predictable; most resets set <code>*, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box }</code>. Vertical margins between blocks can collapse.`,
  t: `Your PDF lists "What is BOM in CSS". BOM usually means the Browser Object Model, a JavaScript concept (window, location, history, navigator). In CSS the related idea is the box model.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between px, em, rem, %, vw and vh?`,
  k: `px is a fixed size; em and rem grow with the text size; % depends on the parent; vw and vh depend on the screen size.`,
  a: `<code>px</code>: absolute CSS pixel. <code>em</code>: relative to the element's own font-size (for font-size, the parent's), so it compounds when nested. <code>rem</code>: relative to the root (html) font-size, consistent and good for type and spacing that respects user settings. <code>%</code>: relative to the parent. <code>vw/vh</code>: 1% of viewport width/height (use <code>dvh</code>/<code>svh</code> on mobile). Also <code>ch</code>, <code>fr</code> (grid), and <code>clamp()</code> for fluid sizes.` },
{ q: `What is flexbox?`,
  k: `Flexbox lines boxes up in a row or column and spaces them nicely, even when the screen changes size.`,
  a: `A one-dimensional layout model. Container: <code>display: flex</code>, <code>flex-direction</code>, <code>justify-content</code> (main axis), <code>align-items</code> (cross axis), <code>flex-wrap</code>, <code>gap</code>. Items: <code>flex-grow</code>, <code>flex-shrink</code>, <code>flex-basis</code> (shorthand <code>flex: 1</code>), <code>align-self</code>, <code>order</code>. Use <b>Grid</b> for two-dimensional layouts (rows and columns together).`,
  c: `.toolbar { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; gap: 12px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
.center { display: grid; place-items: center; }` },
{ q: `How do you make a website responsive?`,
  k: `Make the page stretch and rearrange itself so it looks good on a phone, a tablet and a big screen.`,
  a: `Viewport meta tag; mobile-first CSS; fluid layouts with flexbox/grid, %, <code>fr</code>, <code>minmax</code>, <code>auto-fill</code>; media and container queries; relative units (rem) and <code>clamp()</code> for type; responsive images (<code>max-width: 100%</code>, <code>srcset</code>, <code>sizes</code>, <code>&lt;picture&gt;</code>); touch-friendly targets (~44px); test on real devices and DevTools device mode.` },
{ q: `What are common breakpoints for responsive design?`,
  k: `Sizes where the layout changes: phone, tablet, laptop and big screen.`,
  a: `Common choices: ~480px (small phones), 640px, 768px (tablets), 1024px (small laptops), 1280px, 1536px (large desktops). Bootstrap uses 576/768/992/1200/1400; Tailwind uses 640/768/1024/1280/1536. Best practice: set breakpoints where your content breaks, not for specific devices.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between CSS Grid and Flexbox?`,
  k: `Flexbox arranges a single line; Grid arranges a whole chessboard.`,
  a: `Flexbox is one-dimensional and content-driven: great for toolbars, nav bars and centring. Grid is two-dimensional and layout-driven: page layouts, card grids, aligning across rows and columns, named areas. They combine well: grid for the page, flex inside components.` },
  ],
});

HUB.add({
  id: 'reactlist', title: 'React: my interview list', short: 'My React list',
  blurb: 'Every React question from your PDF, answered in your list\'s own words.',
  items: [
{ q: `What is React JS?`,
  k: `A box of Lego bricks for building web pages; when something changes, only the changed bricks are swapped.`,
  a: `A JavaScript library from Meta for building user interfaces with reusable <b>components</b>. You describe the UI for a given state declaratively; React re-renders and reconciles changes to the DOM efficiently. Key ideas: JSX, components, props and state, one-way data flow, hooks. Current major version: React 19.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between the virtual DOM, the shadow DOM and the real DOM?`,
  k: `Real DOM: the actual page. Virtual DOM: React's sketch of the page that it compares before changing the real one. Shadow DOM: a private room inside one element whose styles don't leak out.`,
  a: `<b>DOM</b>: the browser's tree of the page; updating it triggers layout and paint, so frequent changes are costly. <b>Virtual DOM</b>: React's in-memory tree of elements; on updates React diffs the new tree with the old (reconciliation) and applies minimal DOM changes. <b>Shadow DOM</b>: a browser feature for Web Components that encapsulates a subtree's DOM and styles (<code>attachShadow</code>); unrelated to React's virtual DOM.`,
  t: `Your PDF says "shallow dom"; the standard term is shadow DOM. Don't confuse it with shallow rendering (an old Enzyme testing feature) or shallow copy.` },
{ q: `What are controlled and uncontrolled components?`,
  k: `Controlled: React holds the puppet strings of the input. Uncontrolled: the input looks after itself and you check it later.`,
  a: `Controlled inputs keep their value in React state (<code>value</code> + <code>onChange</code>): easy validation and conditional UI. Uncontrolled inputs keep the value in the DOM, read via refs or <code>FormData</code> (<code>defaultValue</code>): less code, fewer renders, used by React Hook Form and React 19 form actions. File inputs are always uncontrolled.` },
{ q: `What are hooks in React?`,
  k: `Magic stickers that give function bricks memory and superpowers.`,
  a: `Functions starting with <code>use</code> that let function components use state, effects, context, refs and more: <code>useState</code>, <code>useEffect</code>, <code>useContext</code>, <code>useReducer</code>, <code>useMemo</code>, <code>useCallback</code>, <code>useRef</code>, <code>useLayoutEffect</code>, <code>useId</code>, <code>useTransition</code>, plus React 19's <code>use</code>, <code>useActionState</code>, <code>useOptimistic</code>. Rules: call at the top level, only from components or custom hooks.` },
{ q: `What are JSX, Babel and Webpack?`,
  k: `JSX is how you draw the page in code; Babel translates it so browsers understand; Webpack packs all the files into boxes.`,
  a: `<b>JSX</b>: HTML-like syntax in JavaScript that compiles to <code>jsx()</code> calls producing React elements. <b>Babel</b>: a compiler that transforms JSX and modern JS into code target browsers support (SWC and esbuild do this faster today). <b>Webpack</b>: a bundler that builds a dependency graph, runs loaders (Babel, CSS) and outputs optimised bundles. Modern React apps commonly use <b>Vite</b> (esbuild/Rollup) instead.` },
{ q: `What is Redux?`,
  k: `One big cupboard for the whole app; you change it only by sending notes that a strict librarian reads.`,
  a: `A predictable state container: global state in one store, changed only by dispatching plain-object actions, with pure reducers computing the next state. Written today with <b>Redux Toolkit</b>; server data is better handled by RTK Query or TanStack Query.` },
{ q: `What are reducer, action and store in Redux?`,
  k: `Action: a note saying what happened. Reducer: the rule book. Store: the cupboard.`,
  a: `<b>Action</b>: <code>{ type: 'cart/itemAdded', payload }</code> describing an event. <b>Reducer</b>: pure function <code>(state, action) =&gt; newState</code>. <b>Store</b>: holds the state tree; exposes <code>getState</code>, <code>dispatch</code>, <code>subscribe</code>; created with <code>configureStore</code>.` },
{ q: `What is middleware in Redux?`,
  k: `A checkpoint between sending a note and the librarian reading it, where helpers can log it or do shop trips.`,
  a: `Functions wrapping <code>dispatch</code> (<code>store =&gt; next =&gt; action =&gt; ...</code>) to log, transform, delay or run async logic before actions reach reducers. Examples: thunk (default in RTK), listener middleware, RTK Query middleware, saga, logger.` },
{ q: `Explain the data flow in Redux.`,
  k: `Click → send a note → the rule book makes a new cupboard → bricks that care redraw.`,
  a: `Unidirectional: UI event → <code>dispatch(action)</code> → middleware (optional async work) → reducers compute new state → store saves it and notifies subscribers → <code>useSelector</code> re-runs selectors → components whose selected data changed re-render.` },
{ q: `What is Redux Thunk?`,
  k: `A helper that lets you send a "do this errand" job instead of a note; the job can wait for the shop and then send real notes.`,
  a: `Middleware that lets you dispatch functions <code>(dispatch, getState) =&gt; {}</code> for async or conditional logic, dispatching plain actions along the way. Built into Redux Toolkit; <code>createAsyncThunk</code> wraps it with pending/fulfilled/rejected actions.` },
{ q: `What is Redux Saga, and how is it different from Redux Thunk?`,
  k: `Thunk is a simple errand runner; Saga is a manager who watches every note and runs long, complicated errands step by step.`,
  a: `Saga uses generator functions and declarative effects (<code>call</code>, <code>put</code>, <code>takeLatest</code>, <code>race</code>) for complex flows with cancellation and easy testing. Thunk uses plain async functions: smaller and simpler, but complex orchestration gets messy. Default to thunk/RTK (or RTK listener middleware); use saga only for complex long-running workflows or existing codebases.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between class components and function components?`,
  k: `Class bricks are old robots with "this"; function bricks are recipes with magic stickers.`,
  a: `Classes use <code>this.state</code>, <code>setState</code> and lifecycle methods; functions use hooks. Functions are shorter, reuse logic via custom hooks, avoid <code>this</code> binding issues, and support all modern React features (Compiler, Server Components). Classes are still needed only for error boundaries (or use a library).` },
{ q: `How do you implement componentWillUnmount in a function component?`,
  k: `Return a clean-up job from useEffect; React runs it when the brick is put away.`,
  a: `Return a cleanup function from <code>useEffect</code> with an empty dependency array. Cleanup also runs before the effect re-runs when dependencies change.`,
  c: `useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(tick, 1000)
  window.addEventListener('resize', onResize)
  return () => {                 // componentWillUnmount
    clearInterval(id)
    window.removeEventListener('resize', onResize)
  }
}, [])` },
{ q: `Explain useState, useEffect, useMemo and useCallback in detail.`,
  k: `useState: memory. useEffect: jobs after drawing. useMemo: remember a hard answer. useCallback: keep the same instruction card.`,
  a: `<b>useState</b>: <code>[value, setValue]</code>; setting schedules a re-render; use the updater form for values depending on the previous one; lazy initialiser for expensive setup. <b>useEffect</b>: sync with external systems after paint; deps control when it re-runs; return cleanup. <b>useMemo</b>: cache an expensive computed value or keep an object reference stable until deps change. <b>useCallback</b>: cache a function reference for memoized children or hook deps. With the React Compiler, manual useMemo/useCallback is rarely needed.`,
  c: `const [count, setCount] = useState(0)
useEffect(() => { document.title = 'Count ' + count }, [count])
const sorted = useMemo(() => [...items].sort(byName), [items])
const onSelect = useCallback(id => setSelected(id), [])` },
{ q: `Explain the lifecycle methods in React.`,
  k: `A brick is born, changes, and is put away. Each moment has a special place to run code.`,
  a: `<b>Mounting</b>: constructor → getDerivedStateFromProps → render → componentDidMount. <b>Updating</b>: getDerivedStateFromProps → shouldComponentUpdate → render → getSnapshotBeforeUpdate → componentDidUpdate. <b>Unmounting</b>: componentWillUnmount. <b>Errors</b>: getDerivedStateFromError, componentDidCatch. Hooks equivalents: useEffect (mount/update/cleanup), useLayoutEffect, React.memo for shouldComponentUpdate.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between export default and named export?`,
  k: `A default export is the one main toy from a box (you can call it anything). Named exports are labelled toys you must ask for by their label.`,
  a: `A module has at most one <code>export default</code>, imported with any name and without braces. It can have many named exports, imported with matching names in braces (renamable with <code>as</code>). Named exports give better refactoring, autocomplete and tree-shaking clarity; <code>React.lazy</code> requires a default export (or a <code>.then</code> mapping).`,
  c: `// math.js
export const PI = 3.14
export function add(a, b) { return a + b }
export default function greet() { return 'Hello' }
// app.js
import greet, { PI, add as sum } from './math.js'` },
{ q: `What is a portal in React?`,
  k: `A magic door that lets a brick appear somewhere else on the page, like a pop-up on top of everything.`,
  a: `<code>createPortal(children, domNode)</code> renders children into another DOM node (often <code>document.body</code>) while keeping them in the React tree, so context and event bubbling follow React parents. Used for modals, tooltips, toasts and dropdowns that must escape <code>overflow: hidden</code> or z-index stacking.` },
{ q: `What is reconciliation in React?`,
  k: `React compares the new drawing with the old one and only fixes the parts that changed.`,
  a: `The diffing process that decides DOM updates. Heuristics: different element types → tear down and rebuild the subtree; same type → update props and recurse; lists matched by <code>key</code>. This makes updates O(n) instead of a full tree comparison. Fiber (React 16+) makes reconciliation interruptible for concurrent rendering.` },
{ q: `What is useRef in React?`,
  k: `A secret pocket that keeps something between draws without causing a redraw.`,
  a: `<code>useRef(initial)</code> returns a stable <code>{ current }</code> object. Uses: DOM access (focus, scroll, measure), storing mutable values (timer ids, previous values, AbortControllers). Changing <code>.current</code> doesn't re-render. In React 19 you can pass <code>ref</code> as a normal prop to function components.` },
{ q: `What is server-side rendering in React?`,
  k: `The kitchen makes the sandwich before sending it, so you see it at once; then it comes alive in your hands.`,
  a: `Rendering components to HTML on the server per request (faster first paint, better SEO), then hydrating on the client to attach interactivity. React 18+ supports streaming SSR with Suspense; frameworks (Next.js, React Router framework mode) handle it, along with Server Components.` },
{ q: `What is "use strict" / React StrictMode?`,
  k: `"use strict" makes JavaScript stricter about mistakes; StrictMode is a practice mode in React that checks your bricks twice.`,
  a: `<b>"use strict"</b> (JavaScript): opt-in strict mode that turns silent errors into thrown errors, forbids undeclared variables, makes <code>this</code> undefined in plain function calls, and disallows some syntax. ES modules and classes are strict automatically. <b>&lt;StrictMode&gt;</b> (React): development-only checks; double-invokes render, initialisers and reducers, and mounts/unmounts/remounts components so effects run setup → cleanup → setup, exposing impure code and missing cleanup.`,
  t: `Your PDF says "useStrict"; there's no React hook by that name. Interviewers usually mean StrictMode or "use strict".` },
{ q: `What is a Fragment in React?`,
  k: `An invisible bag that groups several bricks without adding an extra box.`,
  a: `<code>&lt;&gt;...&lt;/&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;Fragment key&gt;</code> returns multiple elements without adding a wrapper DOM node, keeping HTML valid (tables, lists) and layouts clean. Use the long form when a key is needed.` },
{ q: `What is React Router?`,
  k: `A map for your app: different addresses show different rooms, without reloading the house.`,
  a: `The standard routing library: maps URLs to components with client-side navigation, nested routes and layouts (<code>Outlet</code>), params (<code>useParams</code>), navigation (<code>Link</code>, <code>useNavigate</code>), and in v7 data loading (loaders/actions) and a framework mode with SSR.` },
{ q: `What is node_modules in a React project?`,
  k: `The big toy storage room where all the borrowed toys (packages) are kept.`,
  a: `The folder where npm/yarn/pnpm installs project dependencies and their dependencies, as declared in <code>package.json</code> and pinned in the lockfile. It's generated, so don't commit it (add to .gitignore); recreate with <code>npm install</code> (or <code>npm ci</code> in CI). Bundlers resolve imports from it.` },
{ q: `What is the default localhost port in React, and how do you change it?`,
  k: `The practice shop on your computer opens at a door number; you can choose a different door.`,
  a: `Vite dev server: <b>5173</b> (change with <code>server.port</code> in <code>vite.config.js</code> or <code>vite --port 3000</code>). Create React App and Next.js: <b>3000</b> (CRA: <code>PORT=4000 npm start</code> or a <code>.env</code> file; Next.js: <code>next dev -p 4000</code>).`,
  c: `// vite.config.js
export default defineConfig({ plugins: [react()], server: { port: 3000 } })` },
{ q: `What is a higher-order component (HOC)?`,
  k: `A gift wrapper: put a brick inside and get back the same brick with extra powers.`,
  a: `A function that takes a component and returns a new component with added behaviour (auth checks, logging, injected data): <code>const Enhanced = withAuth(Profile)</code>. Forward props, set <code>displayName</code>, don't create HOCs during render. Custom hooks replace most HOCs today.` },
{ q: `What is a pure component?`,
  k: `A brick that only redraws if its instructions really changed.`,
  a: `<code>React.PureComponent</code> (classes) implements <code>shouldComponentUpdate</code> with a shallow comparison of props and state. The function equivalent is <code>React.memo</code>. Also "pure" in the functional sense: same props and state always render the same output with no side effects, which React requires of all components.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between state and props?`,
  k: `Props are what your parents tell you; state is what you remember yourself.`,
  a: `Props are read-only inputs passed from parent to child. State is data owned and updated by the component. Both trigger re-renders. Lift state up when siblings need to share it, and pass it down as props.` },
{ q: `How do you optimise a React app?`,
  k: `Redraw less, carry less, and don't fetch the same toys twice.`,
  a: `Measure with the Profiler and Web Vitals first. Then: colocate state, split context, memoize (or React Compiler), virtualize long lists, code-split routes with <code>lazy</code>, analyse and trim bundles, debounce inputs and use <code>useTransition</code>, cache server data with TanStack Query/RTK Query, avoid request waterfalls, optimise images and fonts, and use SSR/SSG where it helps.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between React and Angular?`,
  k: `React is a box of bricks you combine with your own tools; Angular is a full house kit with every tool included.`,
  a: `<b>React</b>: UI library, JSX, one-way data flow, choose your router/state/data tools, hooks, huge ecosystem, React Native for mobile. <b>Angular</b>: full framework by Google with TypeScript first, templates, dependency injection, router, forms, HTTP client and CLI built in, RxJS, and Signals for reactivity in recent versions. React is more flexible; Angular is more opinionated and consistent across large teams. (AngularJS 1.x is the old, end-of-life predecessor.)` },
{ q: `What is prop drilling, and how do you overcome it?`,
  k: `Passing a note hand to hand through many people who don't need it, just to reach the last person.`,
  a: `Passing props through intermediate components that only forward them. Fixes: component composition (pass <code>children</code> or elements), Context for widely used values, a state library (Zustand, Redux) for frequently changing shared state, and moving server data to a query cache so components fetch what they need.` },
{ q: `What is the Context API?`,
  k: `A loudspeaker: say it once at the top and every child in the house can hear.`,
  a: `<code>createContext</code> + a provider + <code>useContext</code> (or <code>use</code>) to share values like theme, locale and auth without prop drilling. Consumers re-render when the value changes, so memoize the value and split contexts by update frequency.` },
{ q: `What are super, constructor and render in React class components?`,
  k: `constructor sets up the robot when it's born, super lets the parent robot set itself up first, and render draws the picture.`,
  a: `<code>constructor(props)</code> runs once before mount to initialise state and bind methods. <code>super(props)</code> calls the parent <code>React.Component</code> constructor; it must be called before using <code>this</code> (passing props makes <code>this.props</code> available inside the constructor). <code>render()</code> returns JSX, must be pure, and runs on every update. Class fields (<code>state = {}</code>, arrow methods) remove the need for a constructor.`,
  c: `class Counter extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props)
    this.state = { count: 0 }
    this.increment = this.increment.bind(this)
  }
  increment() { this.setState(s => ({ count: s.count + 1 })) }
  render() { return <button onClick={this.increment}>{this.state.count}</button> }
}` },
  ],
});
